module newtype.neg_1

// ONE seeded cause: the constructor is given something that is not the
// representation. `Row` wraps an `int`, and a string is not one.
//
// novac refuses it BY NAME, at the argument. The ORACLE does not: `nova check`
// accepts this file and clang then refuses the emission -- class O-1c (the
// checker admits what its own codegen rejects), measured 2026-08-21 and
// registered in docs/dev/novac-divergences.md. `Row(1, 2)` -- two arguments to a
// one-field wrapper -- passes its check too. So this fixture asserts novac's
// refusal, not an agreement; the differential guard judges `pos_*` files only,
// which is why the divergence lives in the registry rather than here.
//
// What the refusal replaced matters more than its wording. Until 2026-08-21
// EVERY type in call position -- this mistake and the legal `Row(7)` alike --
// reached the lattice and came back "internal compiler error (novac bug, not
// yours)". An ICE on a user's mistake is the P12 class; an ICE on a LEGAL form
// is worse, and both were here.
type Row int

fn main() {
    ro r = Row("s")
    println(1)
}
